1) Take the container identifier from the alert labels and confirm the growth of nr_throttled and throttled_usec with cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat;
2) Compare actual CPU usage against the quota; being throttled while below the quota usually means bursts are being sliced up by the scheduling period;
3) JVM and Go applications must set -XX:ActiveProcessorCount or GOMAXPROCS to match the CPU quota, otherwise excess threads make throttling worse;
4) Once the workload is confirmed reasonable, raise the CPU quota and watch the wait ratio fall.

1) 执行 cat /proc/net/sockstat 与 sysctl ne:
1) Run cat /proc/net/sockstat and sysctl net.ipv4.tcp_mem to compare current usage against the three watermarks;
2) Run ss -s to count connections, and if the count is abnormally high, find the owning process (ss -tanp aggregated with awk);
3) If many connections are backing up their receive or send queues, handle it via the backlog-drop path and fix consumption speed;
4) If this is genuine business scale, raise the three net.ipv4.tcp_mem watermarks, keeping the total to a sensible fraction of physical memory.

1) 执行 dmesg -T | grep -i 'killed process:
1) Run dmesg -T | grep -i 'killed process' to identify the killed process and its memory usage at the time;
2) Run ps aux --sort=-rss | head -15 to see the current ranking and whether anything is still growing;
3) If a single process is abnormal, investigate it as an application memory leak; if several processes are competing, the host is oversubscribed and some workload must be moved;
4) You can lower a critical process's oom_score_adj to make it less likely to be killed, but that only relieves the symptom and the total memory problem must still be solved.

1) 执行 mtr 到主要对端地址，定位丢包发生在哪一跳；\n2) 丢包在本机出:
1) Run mtr to the main peer addresses to identify which hop is losing packets;
2) If the loss is on this host's egress, check NIC error and drop counters (ethtool -S <nic> | grep -iE 'err|drop') and the queue configuration;
3) If the loss is on an intermediate link, escalate to the network team and keep the mtr output as evidence;
4) If retransmissions are high even within the same data centre, focus on switch port errors and bandwidth utilization, which usually means link quality or congestion.

1) 执行 netstat -s | grep -iE 'listen|SYN':
1) Run netstat -s | grep -iE 'listen|SYN' to see the detailed drop and SYN statistics;
2) If the SYN queue is full, raise net.ipv4.tcp_max_syn_backlog and confirm net.ipv4.tcp_syncookies=1 so connections can still be established under a flood;
3) Read this together with the accept queue overflow alert; they often appear together and usually share the root cause of an application accepting too slowly;
4) If you suspect a SYN attack, aggregate the sources from ss -tan state syn-recv and rate-limit at the firewall.

1) 执行 ss -lnt 对比 Send-Q（队列上限）与 Recv-Q（当前:
1) Run ss -lnt and compare Send-Q (the queue limit) with Recv-Q (the current backlog) to find the overflowing listening port;
2) If the limit is too small, raise the application's listen() backlog and raise the kernel's net.core.somaxconn to match (the smaller of the two applies);
3) A permanently full queue means the application is accepting too slowly; check whether it is blocked in business logic instead of accepting, or whether its thread pool is saturated;
4) Confirm net.ipv4.tcp_abort_on_overflow stays 0; setting it to 1 returns an RST immediately, which makes the problem more visible but also more severe.

1) 执行 ss -tanm 查看各连接的接收队列与内存占用，找出 Recv-Q:
1) Run ss -tanm to inspect per-connection receive queues and memory usage and find the connections whose Recv-Q stays backed up;
2) Once you have the owning process, check whether it is blocked in business processing rather than reading; add threads or switch to asynchronous reads;
3) Raising net.core.rmem_max and the application's SO_RCVBUF helps absorb bursts, but the real fix is consuming faster;
4) Check CPU and GC to confirm the process is not simply paused too long to read.
